Subject: [PATCH 11342/11362] Operator decision: keep the force-turn closure;
 list_transitions stays

The question was whether list_transitions should remain in grammar mode with the
force-turn closure, or be removed so no argument-less envelope exists at any turn.
Operator kept the closure.

The entry describes the mechanism from source rather than from an agent report:
two grammars are frozen per session, and at the force turn the loop swaps in one
whose only admissible envelope is a submission. Before round 2 that turn was a
prompt string; a model can decline a sentence and cannot emit a token the grammar
forbids.

Keeping it is coherent because list_transitions takes no arguments, so {} is its
complete call rather than a degenerate one -- right during inspection, impossible
at the force turn. Removing it would buy a guarantee the closure already gives at
the only turn that matters, and would cost arm comparability by giving the grammar
arm a different tool set from the control.

Records what this does not settle: whether the model submits before the closure
compels it. Trial 2 measures exactly that, with the force turn at the loop default
of 3.

