+## 2026-09-06 00:35Z — OPERATOR DECIDED: keep the force-turn closure
+
+The open question was whether `list_transitions` should stay in grammar mode with the force-turn
+closure, or be removed so no argument-less envelope exists at any turn. **Operator: keep the
+force-turn closure.** `list_transitions` stays; the round-2 design is settled on this point.
+
+**What the closure is, read from source rather than from a report.** Two grammars are built per
+session, both frozen from a deep copy (REQ-ARC-WMTE-6770):
+
+    grammar_text        = _tool_grammar(schemas)                                     # every tool
+    grammar_submit_text = _tool_grammar(schemas, allowed=("run_engine_on_transitions",))
+
+Inspection turns get the full grammar. At the force turn the loop swaps in the second, whose only
+admissible envelope is a submission. The code comment states the reason exactly: "At the force turn
+the prompt nudge alone is a request the model may ignore. Under grammar transport the loop can make
+it a constraint: only a submission is admissible."
+
+Before round 2 the force turn was `_FORCE_ENGINE_NUDGE`, a prompt string. A model can decline a
+sentence; it cannot emit a token the grammar forbids.
+
+**Why keeping it is coherent rather than a concession.** `list_transitions` takes no arguments, so
+`{}` is its COMPLETE and correct call, not a degenerate one. The empty envelope is the right shape
+for that tool during inspection and is physically impossible at the force turn. The alternative —
+removing the tool from grammar mode — would buy a guarantee the closure already provides at the
+only turn where it matters, and would pay for it by giving the grammar arm a different tool set
+from the control, so the two arms would differ by more than the transport and every comparison
+between them would weaken.
+
+**What this does NOT settle.** Whether the model submits before the closure compels it. Trial 1
+could not tell: its force turn fired at 2 and every cell submitted only afterwards. Trial 2 is
+running now with the force turn at the loop default of 3, on Opus 5 per operator instruction, and
+reports per cell the turn index of the first submission against the force turn. A submission at
+turn 1 or 2 is unprompted; at turn 3 it is compelled and adds nothing.
+
+If the answer is that the 27B never submits unprompted, the honest reading is that the grammar's
+value is transport efficiency — 4.7x fewer decode tokens, 3.1x faster in trial 1 — rather than
+autonomous tool use. That is worth having on its own terms and should not be dressed as something
+else.
+
